{#
/**
 * @file
 * The visible trigger for a description that was moved into a tooltip.
 *
 * Included rather than repeated, because a field's name is drawn in four
 * different places — a label, a legend, and a details summary that three
 * templates override — and a trigger missing from one of them is a field whose
 * help nothing on screen admits to.
 *
 * Available variables:
 * - neo_tooltip_help: Unset when this field has no tooltip, or when the trigger
 *   belongs somewhere else. Otherwise carries:
 *   - label: the accessible name for the trigger.
 *   - target: empty when the trigger sits with the control it describes, and
 *     otherwise the id of the control whose tooltip it opens — a control with
 *     no visible label of its own is named by the group around it, so its
 *     trigger is drawn on that group's legend or summary instead.
 *   - attributes: (optional) tooltip attributes, set only where this button is
 *     the whole trigger rather than a second one.
 *
 * Usually no tooltip attributes: js/tooltip.ts adds this button to the
 * triggerTarget of the instance already bound to the field, so the trigger and
 * the field open one tooltip rather than two carrying the same sentence. The
 * exception is a field named by a table header, where there is no control
 * holding an instance to join — there this button carries its own.
 *
 * A button, not the <a href="" onclick="return false"> the tooltip builder
 * reaches for elsewhere: this has to be reachable by Tab and operable on touch,
 * where there is no hover to reveal the tooltip at all, and a button is both
 * without any script to cancel a navigation.
 *
 * @see neo_tooltip_preprocess_form_element_label()
 * @see neo_tooltip_preprocess_fieldset()
 * @see neo_tooltip_preprocess_details()
 */
#}
{% if neo_tooltip_help %}
  {#
    One attribute object, not a literal `class` beside a printed one: the
    tooltip attributes carry `use-neo-tooltip`, and a second `class` attribute
    on the same tag is dropped by the parser rather than merged — which left the
    badge looking right and never opening anything.
  #}
  {%
    set help_attributes = (neo_tooltip_help.attributes|default(create_attribute()))
      .setAttribute('type', 'button')
      .setAttribute('data-neo-tooltip-help', neo_tooltip_help.target)
      .setAttribute('aria-label', neo_tooltip_help.label)
      .addClass('form-label-help')
  %}
  <button{{ help_attributes }}>
    {#
      A character, not an icon-font glyph: `form-label-help` draws the badge and
      centres this inside it, so there is no baseline to correct and nothing to
      render wrong if the icon font never loads. aria-hidden because the
      button's aria-label is the accessible name.
    #}
    <span aria-hidden="true">?</span>
  </button>
{% endif %}
